course: dictionary
lesson: 8288
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some words wear many coats. The word rise can mean go up, get up, increase, or a small hill. Context tells you which meaning is right.
explain: «Smoke rises» means smoke goes up into the air. «Prices rose» means prices went higher. It is the same word, but the words around it show you the meaning.
explain: Rise is also a noun, so you can have «a rise». It can be a small slope on a path, an increase in numbers, or (in the UK) more pay.
explain: Try the swap test: put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Rise means to go up or move upward, as in «Smoke rises».
fact: The Sun rises in the morning when it appears in the sky.
fact: Rise can mean increase, as in «Prices rose».
fact: To rise can also mean to get up from bed or from a chair.
fact: Phrases: «rise and shine» means wake up, «on the rise» means getting bigger, and «give rise to» means cause.
exercise: In «Smoke rises from the chimney», what does rises mean? | *Goes upward | Stops moving | Falls to the ground | Turns into rain
exercise: Mum says, «Rise and shine!» What does she want you to do? | Go to sleep | Eat lunch | Run outside | *Wake up
exercise: In a shop, «Prices rose» this year. What happened to the prices? | They vanished | They went down | They stayed exactly the same | *They went up
test: In a maths sum, «a rise of 5 degrees» means you should do what? | Multiply by 0 | *Add 5 | Divide by 5 | Take 5 away
test: Which meaning fits «a rise in the road ahead»? | A deep hole | A loud noise | A bag of sand | *A small hill or slope
exam: In «Bread rises because of yeast», rise means: | Fall | Get up | Shine | *Go up or grow larger
